WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:06.499
Speaker 1: The nuclear question is increasingly
being asked, I guess, and it's not necessarily

00:00:06.499 --> 00:00:12.309
being answered at this time in the Russian
invasion of Ukraine, but as time goes forward

00:00:12.309 --> 00:00:20.730
and as the takeover of Ukraine does not go
nearly as quickly or nearly as smoothly as

00:00:20.730 --> 00:00:25.420
reports indicate, Vladimir Putin expected
it to go.

00:00:25.420 --> 00:00:33.210
The concern grows that maybe Vladimir Putin
would bring out the nuclear weapons now when

00:00:33.210 --> 00:00:36.620
we talk about nuclear weapons.

00:00:36.620 --> 00:00:43.629
This can mean a lot of different things, and
I think some people imagine, you know, Hiroshima,

00:00:43.629 --> 00:00:44.659
Nagasaki.

00:00:44.659 --> 00:00:50.719
That's not actually what's being considered
here based on what Russia has, and we're going

00:00:50.719 --> 00:00:52.510
to talk about that.

00:00:52.510 --> 00:00:55.310
But that's actually not necessarily a good
thing.

00:00:55.310 --> 00:00:57.800
And I'll explain that in a moment.

00:00:57.800 --> 00:01:04.650
An interesting article from defense one concern
rising that Putin could use nuclear weapons

00:01:04.650 --> 00:01:11.620
Russian escalate to de-escalate doctrine suggests
Putin is thinking the unthinkable.

00:01:11.620 --> 00:01:15.620
And Patrick Tucker, who's the technology editor,
writes the world should take Russia's escalating

00:01:15.620 --> 00:01:17.830
nuclear threats very seriously.

00:01:17.830 --> 00:01:22.210
Senior intel officials told lawmakers Tuesday,
noting they haven't yet seen clear indications

00:01:22.210 --> 00:01:27.630
that Putin would respond to the military setbacks
in Ukraine with nukes.

00:01:27.630 --> 00:01:31.860
Putin sparked alarm among nuclear watchers
last month when he ordered the nuclear forces

00:01:31.860 --> 00:01:36.300
put on a special regime of combat duty.

00:01:36.300 --> 00:01:41.500
And that speaking to lawmakers on Tuesday,
Avril Haines, the director of the Office of

00:01:41.500 --> 00:01:46.670
the Director for National Intelligence, said
the move was mostly signaling to keep Naito

00:01:46.670 --> 00:01:48.130
from intervening.

00:01:48.130 --> 00:01:54.950
However, not everyone is so convinced that
these are these are merely empty nuclear threats.

00:01:54.950 --> 00:01:58.620
And then the question is what do you what
does it mean?

00:01:58.620 --> 00:02:01.480
What would it mean if Russia actually nuked
Ukraine?

00:02:01.480 --> 00:02:08.350
Well, there's a lot of discussion of that,
and there's an article on Heritage Dawg that,

00:02:08.350 --> 00:02:10.289
yes, it's the Heritage Foundation.

00:02:10.289 --> 00:02:12.680
It's an accurate piece.

00:02:12.680 --> 00:02:19.849
Russia's small nukes are a big problem, and
it explains Russia has a 10 to one advantage

00:02:19.849 --> 00:02:26.909
